14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1f",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 16, 1862","place":"Sharpsburg and Keedysville, Maryland, with the Army of Northern Virginia on the ridge between Antietam Creek and the Potomac River and the Army of the Potomac concentrating east of the creek","result":"The armies concentrated for battle without a general engagement on September 16. George B. McClellan postponed his main attack until September 17 while Robert E. Lee strengthened a four-mile defensive line west of Antietam Creek. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson and John G. Walker reached Lee during the day; late in the day, elements of the United States I and XII Corps crossed the creek and made contact with the Confederate left","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The armies at Sharpsburg on Tuesday, September 16, 1862","note":"The episode explains McClellan’s decision not to attack on September 15 or 16, Lee’s decision to stand, and the terrain and Confederate positions. McClellan’s full battle plan is reserved for the next episode."},{"label":"Morning conditions","value":"Heavy fog concealed the Confederate position","note":"At 7 a.m. McClellan informed Henry W. Halleck that the fog had prevented him from determining Lee’s strength and that he would attack after the situation developed."},{"label":"McClellan’s expectation","value":"Lee might have withdrawn during the night","note":"When the fog cleared, the Army of Northern Virginia remained on the heights west of Antietam Creek."},{"label":"Morning force estimate in the episode","value":"About 40,000 United States troops near roughly 15,000 Confederates","note":"These are the episode’s estimates for troops immediately available early on September 16, not final battle strengths. Strength figures vary according to time and whether present, effective, or engaged personnel are counted."},{"label":"McClellan’s estimate of Lee","value":"At least 30,000 at Sharpsburg and 40,000–50,000 at Harpers Ferry","note":"The estimate greatly overstated both parts of Lee’s divided army and made a Confederate reinforcement or flank attack through Pleasant Valley appear more dangerous than it was."},{"label":"Franklin’s position","value":"VI Corps remained in Pleasant Valley for most of September 16","note":"McClellan retained William B. Franklin west of South Mountain to watch for a Confederate move from Harpers Ferry. He ordered the corps toward Sharpsburg at 6 p.m."},{"label":"Jackson as the perceived threat","value":"Reinforce Lee, strike Franklin, or attempt both","note":"McClellan did not know how quickly Jackson could move after the surrender of Harpers Ferry and treated the Confederate wing as a possible threat to either front."},{"label":"United States concentration","value":"Nearly 60,000 troops by the end of September 16 in the episode’s estimate","note":"Most of the Army of the Potomac was within supporting distance by Tuesday evening, apart from Franklin’s VI Corps moving from Pleasant Valley."},{"label":"Confederate concentration","value":"No more than about 25,000 after Jackson’s arrival in the episode’s estimate","note":"Jackson and Walker reached Sharpsburg during September 16. McLaws and A. P. Hill were still returning from Harpers Ferry and would arrive on September 17."},{"label":"Decision by noon","value":"The general assault was scheduled for September 17","note":"After reconnaissance and consideration during the morning, McClellan circulated orders shortly after noon for an attack the next day."},{"label":"Operational consequence of delay","value":"McClellan lost a chance to attack a smaller part of Lee’s army","note":"The delay also allowed the Army of the Potomac to concentrate and placed nearly all of Lee’s army within reach on September 17. This was an unintended consequence, not McClellan’s stated reason for waiting."},{"label":"Lee’s decision","value":"Stand at Sharpsburg instead of withdrawing across the Potomac","note":"Lee sought to recover the initiative after South Mountain and believed McClellan would return to cautious methods. The episode presents this as a calculated risk rather than a position forced by geography alone."},{"label":"Risk to Lee","value":"A divided, depleted army with the Potomac at its back","note":"Confederate troops were exhausted and reduced by straggling and desertion. Boteler’s Ford near Shepherdstown was the principal nearby route of retreat."},{"label":"Defensive line","value":"An arc about four miles long from northwest of Sharpsburg to the lower Antietam","note":"Lee used the ridges west of the creek. Jackson commanded the left, Longstreet the right, and the Hagerstown Turnpike corridor linked the northern battlefield to Sharpsburg."},{"label":"Northern anchor","value":"Nicodemus Heights","note":"J. E. B. Stuart’s cavalry and horse artillery occupied the high ground northwest of the Dunker Church. Jubal A. Early’s infantry brigade also supported this critical sector."},{"label":"Dunker Church plateau","value":"Key high ground at the Hagerstown Turnpike and Smoketown Road","note":"United States artillery placed there could fire along the Confederate line north of Sharpsburg. The church had been built for the German Baptist Brethren in 1852–53 on land donated by Samuel Mumma."},{"label":"Northern approach","value":"A 400–500-yard corridor between the East and West Woods","note":"Miller’s cornfield obstructed much of the open approach north of the church. Hollows, rises, stone outcrops, crops, and woodlots limited observation across terrain that appeared open from the Federal side."},{"label":"Center of the line","value":"The Roulette farm marked the bend between sectors","note":"From Jackson’s left the Confederate position turned south toward D. H. Hill’s and Longstreet’s troops covering the roads and high ground around Sharpsburg."},{"label":"Southern outpost","value":"Robert Toombs’s brigade above the Rohrbach Bridge","note":"The brigade was positioned forward to delay a United States crossing. Snavely’s Ford farther south offered a route around the bridge position."},{"label":"Evening contact","value":"I and XII Corps crossed the upper Antietam and skirmished with Lee’s left","note":"The National Park Service timeline places contact at about 6 p.m. This limited action set the northern approach for the battle but did not become a general engagement on September 16."},{"label":"McClellan’s command test","value":"His first major battle planned and directed from start to finish","note":"Earlier major engagements under McClellan had generally placed the Confederates on the tactical offensive or left detailed battlefield direction to subordinate commanders."},{"label":"Pressure from Washington","value":"Lincoln urged McClellan to destroy Lee’s army if possible","note":"A September 15 telegram congratulated McClellan but emphasized the unfinished task. Winfield Scott’s message the next day likewise treated South Mountain as only part of the campaign."},{"label":"Senior leader casualties in scope","value":"No senior leader was killed or mortally wounded on September 16","note":"Jesse L. Reno’s mortal wound at Fox’s Gap on September 14 remained operationally important because Jacob D. Cox commanded IX Corps at Antietam."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Battle of Antietam: The Bloodiest Day” by Ted Alexander","note":"The episode recommends the Antietam National Battlefield historian’s study for its attention to the battle, Sharpsburg’s civilians, and battlefield preservation."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Halleck, Harpers Ferry, Boteler’s Ford, Nicodemus Heights, Dunker Church, Mumma, Roulette, Rohrbach Bridge, and Snavely’s Ford","note":"The automated transcript produces phonetic forms including “Hallock,” “Harper’s Ferry,” “Bottler’s forward,” “Roulette,” and “Rohrbach.” This guide uses standard historical spellings and “Dunker Church,” the National Park Service form."}],"maps":[{"title":"The armies concentrate at Sharpsburg","caption":"On September 16, Jackson and Walker marched from Harpers Ferry and Shepherdstown to reinforce Lee west of Antietam Creek. Franklin’s VI Corps remained in Pleasant Valley until McClellan ordered it toward Sharpsburg at 6 p.m., while the rest of the Army of the Potomac concentrated from Keedysville. Routes and positions are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield timeline, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep188-september-16-concentration.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of Confederate reinforcements moving from Harpers Ferry and Shepherdstown to Lee at Sharpsburg while the Army of the Potomac concentrates from Keedysville and Franklin’s Sixth Corps remains in Pleasant Valley."},{"title":"Lee’s defensive ground west of Antietam Creek","caption":"Lee’s line curved from Nicodemus Heights and the Dunker Church plateau through the Roulette farm area and Sharpsburg to the high ground above the lower bridge. The Potomac behind the army and Boteler’s Ford to the south made the southern end of the line essential. Terrain and unit footprints are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep188-confederate-defensive-line.svg","alt":"Schematic battlefield map showing Jackson on the Confederate left near Nicodemus Heights and Dunker Church, Longstreet on the right through Sharpsburg, Antietam Creek and its bridges, Snavely’s Ford, and Boteler’s Ford across the Potomac."},{"title":"McClellan observing the battlefield","caption":"Alfred R. Waud’s wartime drawing, titled “McClellan looking at the fight,” shows the Army of the Potomac commander and staff observing Antietam on September 17. It illustrates the elevated Federal vantage discussed in the episode but depicts the battle day, not the reconnaissance of September 16.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep188-mcclellan-looking-at-fight.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud pencil drawing of George B. McClellan and staff on horseback looking across the Antietam battlefield."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:10","audioTime":70,"title":"The September 15 situation is recapped","detail":"Harpers Ferry has surrendered, Lee has chosen to stand at Sharpsburg, and the Army of the Potomac has reached Antietam Creek."},{"time":"3:32","audioTime":212,"title":"Fog hides Lee’s position","detail":"The morning of September 16 opens quietly under heavy fog, and McClellan initially considers whether Lee has withdrawn."},{"time":"4:02","audioTime":242,"title":"McClellan reports to Halleck","detail":"At 7 a.m. McClellan says he cannot yet determine the Confederate strength and will attack after the situation develops."},{"time":"5:07","audioTime":307,"title":"The early numerical opportunity is stated","detail":"The episode estimates about 40,000 United States troops near the field against perhaps 15,000 Confederates early Tuesday."},{"time":"6:16","audioTime":376,"title":"Franklin remains in Pleasant Valley","detail":"VI Corps watches for a Confederate movement from Harpers Ferry instead of joining the main concentration at Sharpsburg."},{"time":"7:16","audioTime":436,"title":"McClellan overestimates both Confederate forces","detail":"He places at least 30,000 with Lee and Longstreet and another 40,000–50,000 with Jackson at Harpers Ferry."},{"time":"9:12","audioTime":552,"title":"Actual concentration is contrasted","detail":"The episode estimates that McClellan has nearly 60,000 by day’s end while Lee has no more than about 25,000 after Jackson arrives."},{"time":"9:26","audioTime":566,"title":"The attack is postponed","detail":"After reconnaissance and deliberation, McClellan circulates orders shortly after noon for a general assault on September 17."},{"time":"9:48","audioTime":588,"title":"Cox expects battle","detail":"Jacob D. Cox, commanding IX Corps after Reno’s death, later remembered September 16 as a day of tedious inaction."},{"time":"10:59","audioTime":659,"title":"The delay’s unintended effect is explained","detail":"Waiting allows both McClellan’s own army and most of Lee’s separated army to concentrate for the battle the next day."},{"time":"12:48","audioTime":768,"title":"Lee’s decision to fight is examined","detail":"The episode presents criticism of Lee’s choice to stand with the Potomac behind him and a badly depleted army."},{"time":"14:41","audioTime":881,"title":"Lee bets on McClellan’s caution","detail":"Lee expects McClellan to return to the deliberate methods that had characterized earlier operations."},{"time":"16:29","audioTime":989,"title":"Lee commits to Sharpsburg","detail":"Despite the risk and his army’s condition, Lee chooses battle in an effort to preserve the campaign’s purpose and initiative."},{"time":"17:12","audioTime":1032,"title":"The battlefield’s ridges and streams are introduced","detail":"The terrain between the Potomac and Antietam Creek appears ordinary from a distance but contains folds, woods, roads, and fields that restrict observation."},{"time":"18:49","audioTime":1129,"title":"The four-mile Confederate arc takes shape","detail":"Lee’s defensive line extends from northwest of Sharpsburg south toward the lower Antietam."},{"time":"18:59","audioTime":1139,"title":"Jackson reaches Sharpsburg","detail":"Jackson arrives around midday after the severe march from Harpers Ferry and takes command of the Confederate left."},{"time":"19:15","audioTime":1155,"title":"Nicodemus Heights anchors the left","detail":"Stuart’s cavalry and horse artillery occupy the high ground, supported by Early’s infantry brigade."},{"time":"19:43","audioTime":1183,"title":"The Dunker Church becomes the key landmark","detail":"The episode locates the church at the Hagerstown Turnpike and Smoketown Road and explains the artillery value of its plateau."},{"time":"20:37","audioTime":1237,"title":"The northern approach is defined","detail":"A narrow open corridor, the East and West Woods, and Miller’s standing corn shape the approach to Jackson’s position."},{"time":"21:29","audioTime":1289,"title":"The line turns south toward Longstreet","detail":"The Roulette farm marks the bend toward the roads and high ground covering Sharpsburg and the lower battlefield."},{"time":"21:57","audioTime":1317,"title":"The lower bridge outpost is described","detail":"A single Confederate brigade guards the heights above Rohrbach Bridge while Snavely’s Ford presents a route around it."},{"time":"23:05","audioTime":1385,"title":"McClellan’s tactical problem is framed","detail":"He must attack ground chosen by Lee with an army whose corps and senior commanders have limited experience operating together."},{"time":"24:20","audioTime":1460,"title":"Antietam becomes McClellan’s command test","detail":"The episode identifies it as the first major battle that he would both plan and personally direct from beginning to end."},{"time":"25:06","audioTime":1506,"title":"Lee’s stand cancels an easy vindication","detail":"McClellan can no longer treat South Mountain alone as proof that the campaign has been won; he must confront Lee again."},{"time":"25:54","audioTime":1554,"title":"Lincoln presses for destruction of Lee’s army","detail":"The president’s telegram blesses McClellan’s success but emphasizes that the Confederate army remains the objective."},{"time":"26:28","audioTime":1588,"title":"Winfield Scott echoes the unfinished task","detail":"Scott’s reply also declines to treat South Mountain as a complete victory while Lee still stands in Maryland."},{"time":"27:19","audioTime":1639,"title":"The book recommendation closes the episode","detail":"The hosts recommend Ted Alexander’s “The Battle of Antietam: The Bloodiest Day.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Reconnoitered Lee’s line on September 16, concentrated the army east of Antietam Creek, and ordered the general attack for the next day"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker","command":"I Corps","subordinates":"Crossed the upper Antietam late on September 16 and moved toward the Miller farm opposite Lee’s left"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","command":"XII Corps","subordinates":"Followed I Corps across the upper creek late in the day to support the opening attack against the Confederate left"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Concentrated east of the Antietam; the corps had camped on and around the Pry farm during the approach"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps","subordinates":"Formed part of McClellan’s concentrated force and remained available near the army center and reserve"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Jacob D. Cox","command":"IX Corps","subordinates":"Exercised corps command after Jesse Reno’s mortal wound at South Mountain; waited east and southeast of the creek for the next day’s orders"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William B. Franklin","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Remained in Pleasant Valley to watch Harpers Ferry until McClellan ordered him toward Sharpsburg at 6 p.m."}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Chose to stand west of Antietam Creek, placed Jackson on the left and Longstreet on the right, and called the remaining Harpers Ferry detachments to Sharpsburg"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Left Wing","subordinates":"Arrived from Harpers Ferry on September 16 and took position from the Dunker Church area toward Nicodemus Heights"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Right Wing","subordinates":"Held the roads, ridges, and approaches around Sharpsburg and extended the defense south toward the lower Antietam"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Hill’s Division, Confederate center","subordinates":"Occupied ground connecting Jackson and Longstreet near the Hagerstown Turnpike, Roulette farm, and roads north of Sharpsburg"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry Division and horse artillery","subordinates":"Held Nicodemus Heights with two cavalry brigades and horse artillery on the extreme Confederate left"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Jubal A. Early","command":"Early’s Brigade, Ewell’s Division","subordinates":"Supported the left near Nicodemus Heights as Lee’s position filled out on September 16"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Robert Toombs","command":"Toombs’s Brigade, D. R. Jones’s Division","subordinates":"Held the forward position above Rohrbach Bridge to delay a United States crossing of the lower Antietam"},{"commander":"Col. Thomas T. Munford","command":"Cavalry on the lower Antietam","subordinates":"Guarded the creek below Sharpsburg toward its junction with the Potomac and the route to Boteler’s Ford"}]}],"people":[{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who postponed the general attack until September 17 while concentrating and reconnoitering","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep188-mcclellan-looking-at-fight.jpg"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who chose to make a defensive stand at Sharpsburg","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander who marched from Harpers Ferry and took command of Lee’s left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate wing commander responsible for Lee’s right and the approaches around Sharpsburg","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander holding the center between Jackson and Longstreet","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William B. Franklin","role":"VI Corps commander retained in Pleasant Valley until ordered to Sharpsburg late on September 16","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jacob D. Cox","role":"United States general exercising IX Corps command after Jesse Reno’s death","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jesse L. Reno","role":"IX Corps commander mortally wounded at South Mountain two days earlier, creating the command vacancy filled by Cox","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"I Corps commander whose troops crossed the upper Antietam and made contact with Lee’s left late on September 16","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph K. F. Mansfield","role":"XII Corps commander ordered across the upper creek in support of Hooker","side":"United States"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Confederate cavalry commander holding Nicodemus Heights and the extreme left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jubal A. Early","role":"Confederate brigade commander supporting Jackson’s left near Nicodemus Heights","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Robert Toombs","role":"Confederate brigade commander assigned to delay a crossing at Rohrbach Bridge","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"United States general in chief who received McClellan’s reports from Sharpsburg","side":"United States"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"United States president who urged McClellan to destroy Lee’s army if possible","side":"United States"},{"name":"Winfield Scott","role":"Retired United States general whose message treated South Mountain as an incomplete victory","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Maj. Gen. Jesse L. Reno","side":"United States","role":"IX Corps commander","outcome":"Mortally wounded through the chest near Fox’s Gap on September 14 and died that evening; his loss explains Jacob D. Cox’s exercise of IX Corps command on September 16"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep188-september-16-concentration.svg","alt":"Schematic map of both armies concentrating around Sharpsburg on September 16, 1862.","caption":"Lee drew Jackson and Walker from the Harpers Ferry operation while McClellan held Franklin in Pleasant Valley and concentrated the remainder of the Army of the Potomac east of Antietam Creek.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield timeline, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep188-confederate-defensive-line.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Lee’s four-mile defensive line west of Antietam Creek.","caption":"Jackson held the northern sector around the Dunker Church and Nicodemus Heights. Longstreet’s right extended through Sharpsburg toward the lower creek, while the road to Boteler’s Ford remained Lee’s essential route across the Potomac.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep188-mcclellan-looking-at-fight.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud pencil drawing of McClellan and staff observing the Antietam battlefield from horseback.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud drew “McClellan looking at the fight” on September 17. The work is a participant-era record of the commander observing the field, one day after the reconnaissance and decisions covered in this episode.","source":"Library of Congress, Morgan collection of Civil War drawings; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep188-dunker-church.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Dunker Church and fallen Confederate artillerymen after the Battle of Antietam.","caption":"Alexander Gardner photographed the Dunker Church after the battle with dead Confederate artillerymen in the foreground. The image records the landmark whose plateau anchored the northern battlefield described in the episode.","source":"Library of Congress, Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 188 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/03/26/civil-war-podcast-episode-188/"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Antietam Campaign timeline, part 2","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/timeline2.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Antietam battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=MD003"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Union Advance Trail background","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/union-advance-trail-guide-background.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: The Pry Farm","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/the-pry-farm.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Dunker Church","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/antietam-battlefield-dunker-church.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: D. R. Miller Farm","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/d-r-miller-farm.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Maryland and Fredericksburg Campaigns, 1862–1863","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo63555/pdf/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo63555.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Antietam order of battle","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/35-3-1.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I: Maryland Campaign reports","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027"},{"label":"Primary source: George B. McClellan’s official campaign reports","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=19"},{"label":"Primary source: Robert E. Lee’s official Maryland Campaign report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=15"},{"label":"Primary source: Thomas J. Jackson’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=41"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Atlas of the battlefield of Antietam","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/map05000006/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Alfred R. Waud, “McClellan looking at the fight”","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660665/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Gardner photograph of the Dunker Church","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018671462/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","McClellan observing the battlefield",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep188-mcclellan-looking-at-fight.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open McClellan observing the battlefield at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep188-mcclellan-looking-at-fight.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud pencil drawing of George B. McClellan and staff on horseback looking across the Antietam battlefield.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"McClellan observing the battlefield"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alfred R. Waud’s wartime drawing, titled “McClellan looking at the fight,” shows the Army of the Potomac commander and staff observing Antietam on September 17. It illustrates the elevated Federal vantage discussed in the episode but depicts the battle day, not the reconnaissance of September 16."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep188-mcclellan-looking-at-fight.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
